"A previous lesson in this unit covered fear's effect of driving qi downward. Chinese medicine actually distinguishes fear from a closely related but separate emotion, shock, which English speakers often flatten into the same word when translating loosely. Fear is traditionally described as a sustained, often anticipated dread — the kind that builds as a threat approaches or lingers after it has passed. Shock is traditionally described as a sudden, unanticipated jolt, with no buildup at all, arising the instant something startling happens without warning.": "05b4bfb79834",
"A previous lesson in this unit covered fear's effect of driving qi downward. Chinese medicine actually distinguishes fear from a closely related but separate emotion, shock, which English speakers often flatten into the same word when translating loosely. Fear is traditionally described as a sustained, often anticipated dread — the kind that builds as a threat approaches or lingers after it has passed. Shock is traditionally described as a sudden, unanticipated jolt, with no buildup at all, arising the instant something startling happens without warning. The distinction matters because the two are traditionally said to scatter qi in different ways. Fear, as covered in the previous lesson, drives qi downward in a directional surge. Shock, by contrast, is traditionally described as scattering qi outward in every direction at once, disorganizing it rather than pushing it toward a single pole — closer to a sudden burst than a steady downward pull, which is why a person who has just been shocked is traditionally described as visibly disoriented and unfocused, in a way distinct from someone gripped by a slower-building fear. Because English commonly uses \"fear\" to cover both feelings, a word-for-word translation risks losing a distinction Chinese medicine treats as clinically meaningful: two different qi patterns, arising from two different kinds of emotional trigger, calling for two different kinds of understanding rather than a single blanket explanation. Recognizing which of the two is actually present is treated as the necessary first step, not an optional refinement.": "7cd75043c44d",
